  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Ledger, Not the Wizard",
    "overview": "**Creative North Star: \"The Ledger, Not the Wizard\"**\n\nFitness Planner speaks Hevy's language with native iOS grammar. Every set of every exercise lives in one scrolling table, read at a glance between sets with a thumb and a short look. Grouped system backgrounds, rounded white cards on the home and history screens, and a flat, edge-to-edge set table during the workout itself. Density comes from columns and hairlines, not from boxes.\n\nColour is restrained: neutrals from the iOS system palette plus one Hevy-blue tint for everything interactive. The only other hues mean set state: green for done, orange for warm-up and records, purple for drop sets, red for failure. Type is SF Pro at Dynamic Type sizes, with tabular figures anywhere a number is compared against another number. Imagery is limited to YouTube thumbnails a plan provides; otherwise exercises are shown as initials.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "One tint (Hevy blue) for every tappable thing, including exercise names.",
      "The set table: SET · PREVIOUS · KG · REPS · ✓, tracked uppercase caption headers, hairline separators, completed rows washed green.",
      "Tabular (monospaced) digits on every figure.",
      "32pt visual controls inside 44pt hit frames.",
      "SF Pro and SF Symbols only; system materials and grouped backgrounds.",
      "Light and dark appearance both supported through system colours plus the two-value tint asset."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The One Tint Rule",
        "body": "Hevy Blue is the only interactive colour. If it is tappable and not a state, it is blue; if it is blue, it is tappable or it marks today/selection.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The State-Only Hues Rule",
        "body": "Green, orange, purple and red never decorate. Each one names a set state (done, warm-up/record, drop, failure) and appears only where that state is true.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Tabular Figures Rule",
        "body": "Every number that can be compared to another (weights, reps, clocks, volumes, dates in the strip) uses monospaced digits so columns hold still as values change.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Dynamic Type Rule",
        "body": "Use text styles, not fixed point sizes. Sizes in the frontmatter are the default (Large) content size; the app scales with the user's setting.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Ledger Rule",
        "body": "Sets are rows in a table separated by hairlines, never cards. One exercise is a header, its notes and rest row, the column header, its set rows, and an Add Set button, in one continuous list.",
        "section": "layout"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Flat Ledger Rule",
